course: arts
lesson: 34
title: Buildings and architecture
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Architecture is the art of designing buildings: an architect draws the plans, engineers check the building will stand up, and a big team of builders actually makes it.
explain: Good buildings are made for their place: tall towers sway a little in the wind instead of cracking, buildings in Japan bend and soak up earthquake shaking, and a tower on soft ground needs a strong foundation, the base a building stands on.
explain: Try it with spaghetti and marshmallows: a square frame squashes into a diamond, but add a diagonal stick and it becomes two triangles that hold firm, which is why roofs, bridges and towers are full of triangles.
explain: Taste in buildings changes and is argued: the Eiffel Tower was called ugly when it was new, and grey concrete flats are hated by some and loved by others today.
explain: People say it their own way: "mardy" in the north of England means sulky, "summat" means something, "yaar" in India means mate, "aye" in Scotland means yes, and "wrecked" in Ireland means very tired.
fact: The Burj Khalifa in Dubai is about 828 metres tall and opened in 2010; very tall buildings are designed to sway a little in the wind, which keeps them safe.
fact: When the Eiffel Tower was built for the 1889 World's Fair, many artists and writers protested that it was ugly; it was meant to stand only 20 years but was kept, partly because it was useful for radio.
fact: Saint Sophia's Cathedral in Kyiv was built in the 11th century, about a thousand years ago, long before the United States was founded in 1776.
fact: The Great Wall of China is very long but only about as wide as a road, so it cannot be seen from the Moon with the naked eye.
fact: The Taj Mahal in Agra, India, was built in the 1600s by the emperor Shah Jahan as a tomb for his wife Mumtaz Mahal, not as a palace to live in.
fact: The Sydney Opera House, designed by the Danish architect Jørn Utzon and opened in 1973, is an arts centre whose shell roofs are often compared to sails.
fact: The Leaning Tower of Pisa began to lean while it was being built in the 1100s, because the soft ground under one side sank; engineers have since made the lean a little smaller to keep it safe.
fact: Many concrete blocks of flats were built from the 1950s to the 1970s to give many people homes quickly; whether they are ugly or bold and beautiful is argued.
fact: Japan has strict earthquake rules, and many buildings have flexible frames or shock-absorbing bases that let them move without breaking.
fact: A square frame can squash sideways, but a triangle cannot change shape without breaking a side, so a diagonal brace makes a square strong.
fact: An architect designs a building and works with engineers; builders, electricians, plumbers and many others build it.
exercise: Ahmed thinks the Burj Khalifa must wobble and fall. Which is true? | It sways a little on purpose | It is about to fall down any day now, and engineers are very worried | Tall buildings never move at all, not even a tiny bit in a storm | It is held by cables
exercise: How tall is the Burj Khalifa? | About 828 metres | About 82 metres | About 8 kilometres | About 8,000 metres
exercise: Chloé says everyone always loved the Eiffel Tower. What is true? | Many artists first called it ugly | It was loved by every single person in Paris from the very first day | It was built only last century to be a radio mast and nothing else | It was built in 1950
exercise: Why was the Eiffel Tower kept after 20 years? | It was useful for radio | Because it was far too heavy for anyone to take apart and move | Because the artists who protested changed their minds and asked to keep it | Because it turned out to be made of gold and was too valuable to scrap
exercise: Yaroslav's cousin asks if St Sophia's is older than America. Is it? | Yes, about a thousand years old | No, it was built some time after America was founded in 1776 | No, it was only built last century, after the Second World War | They are the same age
exercise: Ming's uncle says you can see the Great Wall from the Moon. Which is true? | No, it is too narrow | Yes, it is the only thing made by people that you can see from there | Yes, you can see it from the Moon, but only on a clear day | No, but only because it is painted grey and blends into the land
exercise: Priyanka says the Taj Mahal is a tomb. What is true? | It was built as a tomb | It was a palace where the kings and queens of India lived | It was a school where young princes learned to read and fight | It was a market
exercise: Grace thinks the Opera House is a boat museum because of its sail roofs. What is it? | An arts centre | A museum full of old sailing boats from the harbour | An office for the ships coming in and out of the harbour | A big shopping centre with cafés right next to the water
exercise: Who designed the Sydney Opera House? | The Danish architect Jørn Utzon | Gustave Eiffel | Shah Jahan | An Australian sailor
exercise: Why does the Tower of Pisa lean? | Soft ground sank on one side | It was built wonky on purpose so that the town would become famous | A big earthquake pushed it over to one side about a hundred years ago | Tourists pushed it over
exercise: Ellie says the grey concrete flats are ugly. Which is fair? | Their looks are argued | Everyone in the world agrees they are the ugliest buildings ever made | They were built by people who did not care at all how anything looked | They were built as prisons
exercise: Hiro's friend asks why Tokyo buildings survive earthquakes. Why? | They bend and soak up shaking | They are built very stiff and heavy so they never move at all | Earthquakes never actually hit Tokyo, only the countryside | They float on water
exercise: Nastya's spaghetti squares keep collapsing. What will fix them? | Add a diagonal to make triangles | Use more marshmallows in the corners | Make the squares bigger | Build them faster
exercise: Ciara asks if the architect builds the house himself. What is true? | A big team builds what she designs | The architect builds the whole house alone, brick by brick | The architect only paints it once the builders are finished | The engineer lives in it
exercise: What is an architect? | A person who designs buildings | A person who paints walls | A person who sells houses | A person who drives a crane
exercise: What is a foundation? | The base a building stands on | The top floor of a tower | The roof of a church | The paint on a wall
test: Your mate goes, "If a skyscraper moves even a bit, it's about to fall, innit." What is the best answer? | A little sway is by design | He is right, any movement at all means the building is in danger | Skyscrapers are glued together so that they can never move | Wind cannot reach the top
test: What would happen if you built a tall tower on soft ground with a weak foundation? | It could sink or lean | It would slowly grow taller over time as the ground pushed it up | The soft ground would get harder by itself under the heavy tower | Nothing at all, because foundations do not matter for towers
test: A Gen Alpha kid says, "No cap, the Great Wall is visible from the Moon" ("no cap" means honestly). What is the correction? | It is too narrow | He is right, astronauts on the Moon see it easily every time | It glows at night, so you can see it clearly from the Moon | You can see it, but only if you wear special dark sunglasses
test: In a cartoon, a family moves into the Taj Mahal as their palace. What is wrong? | It is a tomb, not a home | Nothing is wrong, because kings and their families lived there | It is far too small for a whole family to live in comfortably | It is in Pakistan
test: Your cousin in Glasgow says, "Them old buildings are only in Rome, aye?" Which is true? | Kyiv has one about 1,000 years old | Only Rome has any buildings that are more than 500 years old | Every old building in Europe has been knocked down by now | Only Egypt has them
test: A tourist in Pisa says, "They built it wonky on purpose to get famous." What is the honest reply? | Soft ground made it lean | He is right, it was a clever plan to bring tourists to the town | It was pushed over by a big storm only last year | It was built perfectly straight and only leans in photos
test: Your gran in Bolton calls new buildings "dead mardy" (grumpy-looking). Which shows that taste in buildings changes? | The Eiffel Tower was called ugly, then became loved | Every building is loved from day one | People never change their minds | Only old buildings are beautiful
test: Your friend's marshmallow square keeps going wonky. Which shape should she use? | Triangles | Circles, which are the strongest shape for any frame | More squares, stacked higher so they hold each other up | Pentagons
test: Why do some buildings in Japan sit on special bases or bending frames? | To move with the shaking | So they can be picked up and moved to another city later | Because heavy buildings have been banned in Japan by law | To look modern
test: Which is a reason concrete flats were built in the 1960s? | To give many people new homes quickly | To make cities look as grey as possible | To store old cars | Because bricks were banned
test: Who checks that a building is safe to stand up? | Engineers | The first family who moves in and lives there for a year | The painters, when they finish painting the outside walls | The neighbours, who watch it being built from their windows
exam: Identify the principal reason for which very tall buildings are designed to sway modestly in the wind. | Limited flexibility prevents the structure from cracking | Movement indicates imminent collapse | Swaying is required solely for decorative effect | Tall buildings are insensitive to wind
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the visibility of the Great Wall of China from the Moon? | It is too narrow to be seen | It is readily visible as the sole human structure discernible from the Moon | It is visible solely during the hours of darkness owing to its illumination | It is discernible even from Mars
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the inclination of the Tower of Pisa. | It arose during construction owing to the subsidence of soft ground | It was introduced deliberately by the builders to attract fame | It was caused by an earthquake in the present century | It is an illusion produced solely by photography
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a diagonal brace is added to a square frame. | It forms rigid triangles | It increases the weight of the frame for purely decorative purposes | It permits the square frame to fold more readily when required | It replaces the need for any foundation beneath the structure
